   221	We excluded conference proceedings for two main rea-
   222	sons. First, most high-quality secondary studies are pub-
   223	lished in journals. Second, conference proceedings of-
   224	ten lack ISSN numbers, and their quality can vary from
   225	year to year, with some years including colocated work-
   226	shop papers and others not. While this decision may havecaused us to miss some secondary studies, it is unlikely to
   227	have affected the conclusions of this study.
   228	We chose not to use multiple academic databases,
   229	as Scopus already includes metadata from all relevant
   230	databases. However, if our search had been focused
   231	on full-text content, using multiple academic databases
   232	would have been mandatory.
   233	Only secondary studies published between 2013–2023
   234	were included. By narrowing down the publication
   235	year, we wanted to make sure that permanent repository
   236	providers, such as Zenodo and Figshare, are fully in-
   237	cluded.
   238	5. Conclusion and Future work
   239	Our regression model indicates that secondary studies
   240	in software engineering are increasingly adopting open
   241	data practices. In 2023, approximately 62.0% of sec-
   242	ondary studies include a research artifact, a figure we
   243	hope will approach 100% in the future. Only 38.5% of
   244	studies with research artifacts use permanent repositories
   245	with DOIs, which represents another area for improve-
   246	ment. Fortunately, we are also seeing progress in this area,
   247	with the overall average for permanent repositories being
   248	just 12.1% in all SRs, while in 2023 it had already risen
   249	to 30.4%.
   250	The importance of permanent repositories is high-
   251	lighted, as links to non-permanent repositories can be-
   252	come inaccessible. In our study, even among papers pub-
   253	lished in 2023, 2 out of 19 links to non-permanent repos-
   254	itories are already dead.
   255	Regarding 2023, the best adoption is in the papers’
   256	structure itself; 58.2% of all papers contain a dedicated
   257	section stating the availability of data in 2023. Yet, we
   258	found that some papers with their "Data Availability" sec-
   259	tion simply state that “no data was used" or something
   260	to a similar effect. Some papers mention that data is
   261	only “available upon request". Both of these approaches
   262	are alarming because secondary studies rely only on pub-
   263	lished academic studies, and should not encounter confi-
   264	dentiality limitations.
   265	In the future, journals should enforce the reporting
   266	practices of research artifacts to increase the availability
   267	of data. Additionally, identifying which research artifacts
   268	covered in this study have sufficient quality is an impor-
   269	tant future study area.
   270	4
   271	
   272	--- Page 5 ---
   273	Currently, our recommendation to all entities publish-
   274	ing secondary studies is to have a data availability section
   275	as it makes locating the research artifact easier. Addition-
   276	ally, research artifacts should be stored in a permanent
   277	repository with DOIs to ensure long term accessibility.
